# Silent-miscompile prevention guide for x86 backend maintainers

Logline: Defines the silent-miscompile class, catalogs the 8 known instances, and gives three checklists (new emit module, dispatch ladder, CI / smoke) so the next maintainer does not re-introduce the 45.3% empty-stdout rate from audit/x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot.

Setup: openOODA/docs/x86-codegen-style.oot and audit/x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot are read; ooda/oodac/x86_emit_*.oo and ooda/scripts/x86_*_smoke.oo are on disk; 39 emit modules were scored by ooda/scripts/x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo with 37 PASS and 2 FAIL.

Beats:
  1. Name and bound the silent-miscompile class.
  2. Catalog the 8 known instances with file:line evidence and fix status.
  3. Give the 10-item new-emit-module checklist every new module must pass.
  4. Give the 7-item dispatch-ladder checklist the dispatcher must satisfy.
  5. Give the 7 smoke patterns that detect the class in CI.
  6. Record the 5 lessons learned from the v2 hostile probe.

residual: a future lint that statically rejects any x86_emit_*.oo file whose public match fn can return a non-empty value derived from default substitutions; today the audit is a manual read via ooda/scripts/x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo.

# X86 SILENT-MISCOMPILE PREVENTION — Field Guide for the Next Maintainer

File: openOODA/docs/silent-miscompile-prevention.oot
Status: smoke
This guide complements openOODA/docs/x86-codegen-style.oot. The style sheet
teaches you how to write a new emit module. This guide teaches you how to
make sure the new module never silently produces a printing ELF that lies
about what the user wrote.

## 1. Definition

A **silent miscompile** is a backend behavior where the compiler accepts
source code, emits a syntactically valid AT&T `.s` file, and the linked
binary runs to exit code 0, but the binary's runtime behavior does not
match the source: either the binary prints nothing when the source
requires output, prints a hardcoded string the source never asked for,
or executes a body the source did not contain. The class is silent
because no tool in the build chain (lexer, parser, `as`, `ld`, the
binary's exit code) reports a failure. The user sees a successful
build, a successful run, and wrong output.

## 2. Catalog of known instances

| # | Instance | File:line | Severity | Fix status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **No-op main** (matched shape, empty body, exit 0) | `x86_emit_struct.oo:133-231` (prelude + epilogue, body walks to nothing), `x86_emit_list.oo:102-126`, `x86_emit_opt.oo:184-226` | HIGH (2,595 / 2,607 silent-miscompiles in v2) | OPEN — 580 fixtures still hit per `x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot:106-110`; Tier-0 fix is `x86_emit.oo` refuse instead of emit-zero |
| 2 | **Hardcoded default substitution** in match fn | `x86_emit_free.oo:61-69` returns `["100","200"]` when source has `oo_alloc` but no `oo_write_int`; `x86_emit_opt.oo:165-166` substitutes `"hello_opt"` / `"fallback"` when `opt_some` / `opt_unwrap_or` lack STRING args | HIGH | OPEN — both flagged FAIL in `x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo:30,40`; fix is to `return empty` and let the dispatcher fall through |
| 3 | **Silent fall-through in dispatcher** | v1 form was `x86_emit.oo:75-78` `Err(_) => { }`; v2 form at `:75-78` is the fix `Err(typed_e) => { println(typed_e); process_exit(1); }` | HIGH (root cause of "wrong assembly that still assembles") | FIXED in v2 cycle; v1 audit in `x86-ast-typed-shim.oot:74` |
| 4 | **Hardcoded list-length template** ("20") | v1 only; eliminated by List real lower per `x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot:140` | HIGH (was 7-9% of v1 sample) | FIXED in v2 (List real lower) |
| 5 | **Hardcoded float template** ("22.00") | v1 only; reduced to near-zero per `x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot:140` | MEDIUM (was 2-3% of v1 sample) | FIXED in v2 (Float real const-fold) |
| 6 | **SSA per-block remap duplicate labels** | `x86_ssa_emit_remap.oo` (3,215 fixtures fail with `symbol .Lxss_pi_nz is already defined`) per `x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot:50` | HIGH (92.6% of SSA path) | OPEN — single fix in the remap pass |
| 7 | **SSA forward reference to undefined block** | `x86_ssa_emit.oo` / `x86_ssa_emit_remap.oo` references `.Lmain_block2`, `.Lmain_block3` that are never emitted (3,211 fixtures) per `x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot:51` | HIGH (92.6% of SSA path) | OPEN — same fix as #6 (uniqueness + emit-order) |
| 8 | **Misleading "typed" naming** | `x86_ast_typed.oo:1-14` (Academy header) names the shim "typed-AST dispatch shim" but the chain performs no type checking per `x86-ast-typed-shim.oot:78` | MEDIUM (aspirational name, real-world confusion) | OPEN — rename to `x86_emit_stream_ssa` or land `tc_check` |

## 3. New emit module checklist (10 items)

Every new `ooda/oodac/x86_emit_<feature>.oo` module must pass all 10
items before landing. The model is the existing PASS list in
`ooda/scripts/x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo:17-56` (37 modules pass).

1. **Public match fn returns `List[String]`, empty on shape mismatch.**
   First body line is `let empty: List[String] = list_new();`
   (`x86_emit_int.oo:16`, `x86_emit_struct.oo:55`, `x86_emit_match.oo:86`).
2. **Match fn never substitutes defaults.** Every `return` on partial
   match must be `return empty`. See the FAIL list in
   `x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo:62-63` (`x86_emit_free.oo:61-69`,
   `x86_emit_opt.oo:165-166`) for the anti-pattern.
3. **Empty-source guard at the top of the match fn.**
   `x86_emit_struct.oo:56` `if chars_len(src) == 0 { return empty; }`
   is the model. The dispatcher at `x86_emit.oo:58-61` also guards,
   but a module that relies on the dispatcher alone is fragile.
4. **Match fn scans tokens for `KW_FN main` before walking the body.**
   `x86_emit_int.oo:21`, `x86_emit_struct.oo:139-146`,
   `x86_emit_match.oo:92`. Skipping this check matches the wrong fn.
5. **Emit fn prints the product-floor stamp first.**
   `println("# openOODA x86_64 product floor (AT&T, Linux, as+ld)")`
   at `x86_emit_int.oo:102`, `x86_emit_list.oo:103`,
   `x86_emit_struct.oo:128`, `x86_emit_match.oo:187`.
6. **Emit fn uses the standard ELF prelude and SysV frame.**
   `.section .text`, `.globl _start`, `.globl main`, `.p2align 4`
   (`x86_emit_int.oo:103-105`, `x86_emit_struct.oo:130`);
   `push %rbp` / `mov %rsp, %rbp` / `sub $N, %rsp` and the matching
   epilogue (`x86_emit_int.oo:54-56,96-98`, `x86_emit_struct.oo:133,231`).
7. **Emit fn refuses mid-body shape mismatches with `ERR\tx86_emit\t<msg>`**
   and `process_exit(1)`. `x86_emit_float.oo:157` (float not
   const-foldable), `x86_emit_list.oo:23-26` (list test), and
   `x86_emit_struct.oo:176-177,197-198` (struct field must be int
   literal) are the canonical error sites.
8. **Emit fn is silent on the success path.** No `println("compiling...")`,
   no banner, no `process_exit(0)` after the body. The only `println`
   allowed is the AT&T line itself or an `ERR\t<kind>\t<msg>` hard error.
   See `x86-codegen-style.oot:126-128`.
9. **Dispatcher arm has a length guard before calling the emit fn.**
   `x86_emit.oo:88-90,93-95,128-131,168-171,173-176` all guard with
   `if list_len(f) >= N { x86_emit_<feature>(...); return; }`. A bare
   `let f = x86_match_<feature>(src); x86_emit_<feature>(f); return;`
   fires the emit fn on every input and is the #1 cause of no-op main.
10. **Module appears in `ooda/scripts/x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo` with
    a PASS row.** PASS requires every `return` on partial match to be
    `return empty` / `return list_new()` / `return ""` and zero
    hardcoded body on shape mismatch (`x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo:11`).

## 4. Dispatch ladder checklist (7 items)

The 22-arm token walker in `ooda/oodac/x86_emit.oo:46-209` is the
mainline. Each arm is a 5-line block: match, length-guard, call, return.
The ladder must pass all 7 items.

1. **Every arm has the length guard.** Inspect
   `x86_emit.oo:88-90,93-95,98-100,103-105,108-110,113-115,118-120,
   123-125,128-130,133-135,138-140,143-145,148-150,153-155,158-160,
   163-165,168-170,173-175,178-180,190-193`. A missing guard on any
   arm re-introduces the no-op main class.
2. **Order is most-specific-first.** Float and list are at
   `x86_emit.oo:196-202` and `:125-131` because they accept narrow
   shapes; the catch-all `x86_collect_prints` is at `:207-208` last.
   Putting a broad arm before a narrow one re-introduces instance #2.
3. **`Result` `Err` from the typed shim exits, never falls through.**
   `x86_emit.oo:75-79` is the v2 fix (was v1 silent swallow at `:75-78`
   per `x86-ast-typed-shim.oot:74`). Any revert is a regression.
4. **Catch-all `x86_collect_prints` is the last arm.**
   `x86_emit.oo:207-208`. Anything that comes after it is dead code.
   `x86_emit_linux64([])` at `x86_emit_sys.oo:36-67` emits a no-op
   main when msgs is empty; the walk at `x86_emit_walk.oo:201-211`
   ERRs on `!saw_main`, so the no-op only appears when the
   source compiles through a different arm but emits nothing.
5. **No arm emits a hardcoded list, string, or constant.**
   Every body is built from `x86_tok_t` extractions or, where the
   shape is hardcoded, the body is the documented test case
   (e.g. `x86_emit_heap.oo:71` `heap_alloc_test` is the matched
   shape, not a silent fallback per `x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo:33`).
6. **Float mismatch ERRs at `x86_emit.oo:200-201`, not silent.**
   The match-then-ERR pattern at `:196-201` is the correct shape.
   If a future arm wraps this in a silent `else { ... }`, it
   re-introduces instance #1.
7. **Imports at `x86_emit.oo:16-44` match the arms at `:80-205`.**
   `x86_dispatch_wiring_smoke.oo` (Turn 1 contract gate smoke)
   asserts the import / arm pairing is consistent.

## 5. CI / smoke patterns (7 items)

Each pattern is a real smoke file under `ooda/scripts/` that catches
one slice of the silent-miscompile class.

1. **`x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo`** — 39-file static audit that scores
   each emit module PASS or FAIL based on whether its public match fn
   can return a non-empty result derived from default substitutions.
   Currently 37 PASS / 2 FAIL (`x86_refuse_policy_smoke.oo:58-63`).
   Re-run before every `x86_emit_*.oo` change.
2. **`x86_dispatch_wiring_smoke.oo`** — asserts the contract gate,
   secret gate, and 22-arm chain are still present in `x86_emit.oo`
   and that no C-emit import leaked into the x86 path. Catches a
   silent-fall-through regression at the dispatcher level.
3. **`x86_no_template_smoke.oo`** — Turn-2 lock placeholder that will
   become a no-`x86_emit_*.oo`-imports-in-`x86_emit.oo` gate. Today
   it PASSes unconditionally (`x86_no_template_smoke.oo:19`); Turn 2
   will replace the body with the grep gate.
4. **`x86_hostile_leftover_smoke.oo`** — runs the hostile probe on the
   leftover fixtures that fell out of the dispatch chain. Pairs with
   `audit/x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot` (the 6,936-fixture re-run).
5. **`x86_typed_shim_smoke.oo`** — exercises `x86_emit_stream_typed`
   and asserts the typed-path shim does not silently swallow an
   `Err` (regression of instance #3, the v1 silent fall-through at
   `x86_emit.oo:75-78`).
6. **`x86_ssa_block_remap_smoke.oo`** — asserts the SSA per-block
   remap generates unique labels and emits the label before its
   forward reference. Catches instances #6 and #7 (duplicate labels
   and undefined block references in the SSA path).
7. **`x86_runtime_registry_smoke.oo`** — asserts the single canonical
   runtime blob is appended exactly once and the .globl surface
   matches what every emit module references. Catches a class of
   "two runtimes, one wins" silent mismatch.

The smoke runner is `tests/qa/run.oo` per `x86-codegen-style.oot:232`.
Before landing a change to any `x86_emit_*.oo` module, run all 7
smoke files plus `ooda/scripts/script_lint.oo` and
`openOODA/scripts/governance_enforce.oo` (per FORMAT.oot §3.5).

## 6. Lessons learned (5 items)

1. **Silent miscompile is the worst class of failure.** The build
   passes, the binary runs, the binary exits 0, and the output is
   wrong. No tool in the chain complains. The 45.3% empty-stdout
   rate in `audit/x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot:59` survived 14 prior
   orchestrator fixes because the fixes targeted compile-side
   categories (REFUSED, ASM-FAIL) and missed the run-side count.
2. **Refuse-don't-miscompile is the cheap fix.** A `return empty`
   plus a dispatcher length-guard is 2 lines and converts a silent
   miscompile into a visible ERR. The v2 hostile probe
   (`x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot:144`) confirms the orchestrator's
   14 fixes did not move the silent-miscompile rate; a single
   refuse-instead-of-emmit-zero change at the 5 broken shapes
   (Float, List, struct, Result, 3-arm match) would drop the
   rate to ~10% per `x86-hostile-probe-v2.oot:150`.
3. **Hardcoded body templates are a design smell.** The v1
   "20" (list length) and "22.00" (float) clusters existed
   because the emit fn returned a fixed body when the shape
   partially matched. The fix was to refuse the partial match
   and let the caller re-shape the source, not to keep the
   partial-match code path.
4. **A "typed" name is a contract, not a roadmap.**
   `x86_ast_typed.oo` was named for a future `tc_check` that
   has not landed. Callers opted in for type safety and got
   no type safety (`x86-ast-typed-shim.oot:78`). Until the
   property ships, rename or document the gap.
5. **Sample bias can hide the bug.** v1's 300-fixture probe
   reported 39.3% empty; v2's 6,936-fixture probe reported
   45.3%. The +6.0 pp was a sampling artifact, not a
   regression. Future audits must run the full COMPILED set,
   not a hand-picked subset, before claiming a fix worked.
